WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:03.500
Just like Batman himself, the Joker didn't
arrive fully formed.

00:00:03.760 --> 00:00:08.000
Here's how we got from a one-shot gimmick
villain to the embodiment of madness and mayhem

00:00:08.000 --> 00:00:10.840
and how he was almost completely forgotten
along the way.

00:00:11.440 --> 00:00:16.100
It might be hard to believe these days, but
when he first hit the pages in 1939, Batman

00:00:16.100 --> 00:00:17.760
wasn't exactly an original concept.

00:00:17.990 --> 00:00:22.580
Bill Finger and Bob Kane were heavily influenced
by the Shadow, a pulp vigilante who eventually

00:00:22.580 --> 00:00:25.939
found himself replaced in the pop culture
pantheon by Batman.

00:00:25.939 --> 00:00:29.949
In fact, the story that introduced Batman,
"The Case of the Chemical Syndicate," was,

00:00:29.949 --> 00:00:34.600
to put it charitably, "inspired by" a Shadow
novel called Partners of Peril that had been

00:00:34.600 --> 00:00:36.100
released a few years earlier.

00:00:36.320 --> 00:00:39.640
If you don't want to be charitable, you might
even say it was lifted wholesale.

00:00:39.740 --> 00:00:42.500
Before long, of course, Batman emerged as
his own character.

00:00:42.720 --> 00:00:47.080
Six months after his debut, he got the origin
story that remains unchanged to this day,

00:00:47.080 --> 00:00:51.600
and five months after that, Finger and Jerry
Robinson introduced Robin, who redefined the

00:00:51.600 --> 00:00:54.830
idea of a sidekick and inspired a thousand
knockoffs himself.

00:00:54.980 --> 00:00:58.940
Still, those stories were never exactly subtle
about where they were digging up their ideas.

00:00:59.020 --> 00:01:00.980
Enter German actor Conrad Veidt.

00:01:01.170 --> 00:01:06.780
In 1928, he'd starred in a silent film called
The Man Who Laughs, about a 17th-Century noble

00:01:06.780 --> 00:01:07.700
named Gwynplaine.

00:01:07.909 --> 00:01:12.000
His father had been killed in an iron maiden
by political rivals, and as for Gwynplaine

00:01:12.010 --> 00:01:16.500
himself, they disfigured him by cutting his
mouth into a smile, quote, "so that he might

00:01:16.500 --> 00:01:18.330
laugh forever at his fool of a father."

00:01:18.330 --> 00:01:22.780
Gwynplaine grows up, gets revenge, and actually
gets a much happier ending than he gets in

00:01:22.780 --> 00:01:25.170
the Victor Hugo novel on which the film was
based.

00:01:25.280 --> 00:01:29.020
The important part for the then-new Batman
comics, however, was the striking image of

00:01:29.020 --> 00:01:33.300
a man whose face was permanently twisted into
a grin, forced to smile no matter what emotion

00:01:33.300 --> 00:01:36.640
he was actually feeling something that Veidt
did very well in the film.

00:01:36.640 --> 00:01:41.000
Finger was inspired by that incredible visual,
and brought the idea to Jerry Robinson, thinking

00:01:41.000 --> 00:01:42.420
up a new villain for their comic.

00:01:42.680 --> 00:01:44.560
"Let me tell you about this guy I know, Jack.

00:01:45.760 --> 00:01:46.500
Mean kid.

00:01:46.820 --> 00:01:47.620
Bad seed.

00:01:48.560 --> 00:01:49.260
Hurt people."

00:01:49.760 --> 00:01:50.800
"I like him already."

00:01:51.980 --> 00:01:56.800
Batman #1 hit the shelves in 1940, and kicked
off with one of the Golden Age's most memorable

00:01:56.800 --> 00:02:00.480
images: the Joker, smiling at the reader while
plotting a string of murders.

00:02:00.520 --> 00:02:04.460
Right from that first panel, the Joker was
unsettling, for the same reason that Veidt's

00:02:04.470 --> 00:02:09.000
performance was memorable in the film: a total
disconnect between emotion and expression.

00:02:09.000 --> 00:02:12.020
From page one, in a very literal sense, there's
something off about him.

00:02:12.020 --> 00:02:14.040
And the story, of course, bears that out.

00:02:14.189 --> 00:02:18.240
The Joker racks up a dozen kills in his first
appearance, and when he comes back for his

00:02:18.240 --> 00:02:22.209
second story later in that same issue, there's
a visceral quality to his particular brand

00:02:22.209 --> 00:02:25.800
of violence at odds with the stiff art that's
so common in the Golden Age.

00:02:25.980 --> 00:02:29.500
Clearly, the creators were onto something,
even if they didn't realize it.

00:02:29.500 --> 00:02:33.409
The Joker was originally intended to die at
the end of that first story, like most of

00:02:33.409 --> 00:02:37.420
Batman's early foes did, but editor Whitney
Ellsworth decided to keep him around, and

00:02:37.440 --> 00:02:41.840
the last panel of the Joker's body being hauled
away was changed to have the doctors surprised

00:02:41.840 --> 00:02:42.820
that he'd survived instead.

00:02:43.420 --> 00:02:46.780
It wasn't long until the Joker, as a character,
ran up against a problem.

00:02:46.880 --> 00:02:51.279
With the arrival of Robin, the Batman titles
had started to skew towards a younger audience,

00:02:51.280 --> 00:02:54.340
following the example of extremely profitable
heroes like Superman.

00:02:54.580 --> 00:02:58.400
The Joker was already popular enough to stick
around, but he changed to keep up with the

00:02:58.400 --> 00:03:02.879
times, leaning hard into the goofy clown aesthetic
of his personality rather than the serial

00:03:02.879 --> 00:03:05.269
killer stuff that had been a huge part of
his debut.

00:03:05.269 --> 00:03:08.541
A crucial element that the Joker kept even
through the sillier years was that he was

00:03:08.541 --> 00:03:12.980
never given a real name or a definitive origin
story, which is pretty remarkable.

00:03:12.980 --> 00:03:16.480
Comics back then were dedicated to explaining
every single piece of their mythology, to

00:03:16.680 --> 00:03:20.200
the point where they'd eventually have Batman
encounter the guy who killed his parents,

00:03:20.440 --> 00:03:24.299
and then later encounter the guy who really
killed his parents by hiring the first guy.

00:03:24.299 --> 00:03:28.409
That story also explains where Batman really
got the idea for his costume his dad had a

00:03:28.409 --> 00:03:33.279
bat costume that he wore to a costume party
which had already been explained back in 1939.

00:03:33.279 --> 00:03:36.969
The closest anyone came to "explaining" the
Joker was in "The Man Behind the Red Hood"

00:03:36.969 --> 00:03:37.960
in 1951.

00:03:38.220 --> 00:03:41.100
The thing is, that story doesn't actually
tell us anything new.

00:03:41.300 --> 00:03:43.640
"Do you want to know how I got these scars?"

00:03:45.280 --> 00:03:48.140
We never learn the Red Hood's real name, we
never see his face.

00:03:48.380 --> 00:03:51.879
All we find out is that he's had multiple
identities, and that there are things in his

00:03:51.880 --> 00:03:55.320
past that even Batman, the World's Greatest
Detective, doesn't know.

00:03:55.480 --> 00:03:59.559
Even better, we find out that he was willing
to leap into a vat of toxic chemicals rather

00:03:59.560 --> 00:04:01.000
than be apprehended by the police.

00:04:01.220 --> 00:04:05.160
Even before he's the Joker, he's got a disregard
for human life, even his own.

00:04:05.260 --> 00:04:10.159
As an origin story, "The Man Behind the Red
Hood" only served to make the Joker more mysterious.

00:04:10.159 --> 00:04:12.920
If he wasn't always the Joker, was he even
really the Joker now?

00:04:12.920 --> 00:04:14.820
Were there more identities in his past?

00:04:14.940 --> 00:04:18.100
Those questions would remain a part of the
character for the next 70 years.

00:04:18.860 --> 00:04:22.360
Believe it or not, there was a time when the
Joker just wasn't around in the pages of Batman

00:04:22.370 --> 00:04:26.110
or Detective Comics, but it wasn't because
of a lack of popularity, at least not on a

00:04:26.110 --> 00:04:26.800
large scale.

00:04:26.880 --> 00:04:30.520
There was one audience for whom the Joker
was decidedly unpopular, and while it was

00:04:30.521 --> 00:04:34.550
an audience of exactly one person, it turned
out to be the one that mattered the most:

00:04:34.550 --> 00:04:40.090
Julius Schwartz, who took over editing Batman
and Detective in 1964, was said by coworkers

00:04:40.090 --> 00:04:41.780
to have absolutely hated the character.

00:04:41.800 --> 00:04:46.180
As a result, the Joker's role in the core
Batman titles dropped off dramatically, and

00:04:46.190 --> 00:04:51.210
after the Batman TV show ended in 1969, Schwartz
apparently didn't see any reason to keep him

00:04:51.210 --> 00:04:52.160
around at all.

00:04:52.240 --> 00:04:57.340
For the next four years June 1969 to September
1973, to be precise there weren't any new

00:04:57.340 --> 00:05:01.500
Joker stories in those two comics, and just
two in books Schwartz wasn't editing.

00:05:01.700 --> 00:05:06.160
In 1973, though, Schwartz apparently had a
change of heart, or was convinced that four

00:05:06.160 --> 00:05:09.140
years was long enough to go without a villain
that readers still liked.

00:05:09.240 --> 00:05:13.580
The result was one of the most memorable Batman
comics ever: "The Joker's Five-Way Revenge,"

00:05:13.580 --> 00:05:15.360
by Denny O'Neill and Neal Adams.

00:05:19.580 --> 00:05:21.340
"Wait'll they get a load of me."

00:05:24.760 --> 00:05:28.640
That team would wind up contributing some
pretty huge elements to the Batman mythos

00:05:28.650 --> 00:05:33.990
they co-created Ra's al-Ghul, among others,
and O'Neil would himself spend about 15 years

00:05:33.990 --> 00:05:39.009
as the editor of the Batman comics, bringing
over writer/artist Frank Miller from Marvel

00:05:39.009 --> 00:05:42.580
for an ambitious project about Batman's later
years.

00:05:42.840 --> 00:05:45.860
Even with all that, though, this might be
their most important work.

00:05:45.860 --> 00:05:50.061
It wasn't just a reintroduction of the Joker,
both in-universe and for the readers, it was

00:05:50.061 --> 00:05:53.099
arguably the single story that "created" the
modern Joker.

00:05:53.100 --> 00:05:57.420
As the title suggests, he's out for revenge
not against Batman, but against his own henchmen.

00:05:57.740 --> 00:06:01.800
The story established that Joker had been
imprisoned in and escaped from a hospital

00:06:01.800 --> 00:06:05.780
for the criminally insane rather than a prison,
which would eventually lead O'Neil and Irv

00:06:05.780 --> 00:06:08.240
Novick to create Arkham Asylum eight months
later.

00:06:08.430 --> 00:06:12.120
It also brought back the poison that had left
his victims as smiling corpses back in his

00:06:12.129 --> 00:06:16.320
first appearance, and, crucially, established
that his plans weren't necessarily driven

00:06:16.320 --> 00:06:16.920
by logic.

00:06:17.060 --> 00:06:20.920
Only one of the five henchmen targeted in
the story had actually betrayed him to the

00:06:20.920 --> 00:06:23.099
police, but Joker didn't know which one.

00:06:23.100 --> 00:06:27.380
The solution: kill them all, by poison, bombing,
and even feeding one to a shark.

00:06:27.789 --> 00:06:31.819
This was the story that truly ramped up the
Joker's insanity, and was the foundation on

00:06:31.820 --> 00:06:33.159
which everything else was built.

00:06:33.340 --> 00:06:37.760
If the Joker wasn't Batman's true arch-nemesis
before then, he certainly was after.

00:06:38.400 --> 00:06:42.240
It's worth noting that the revitalization
of the Joker and his increasingly unpredictable

00:06:42.240 --> 00:06:45.210
madness wasn't just a product of O'Neil and
Adams.

00:06:45.210 --> 00:06:50.430
A few years later, in 1978, Steve Englehart
and Marshall Rogers, another creative team

00:06:50.430 --> 00:06:54.980
hired away from Marvel, knocked another definitive
Joker story out of the park: "The Laughing

00:06:54.980 --> 00:06:55.480
Fish."

00:06:55.600 --> 00:06:59.740
For his part, Rogers' work here is considered
some of the all-time best Batman art, with

00:06:59.740 --> 00:07:00.320
good reason.

00:07:00.500 --> 00:07:04.960
Unlike a few other artists, he plays the smile
as a permanent fixture on the Joker's face

00:07:04.960 --> 00:07:08.300
rather than just an expression, hearkening
back to Veidt and The Man Who Laughs.

00:07:08.420 --> 00:07:12.699
At heart, it was an updated retelling of the
first Joker story from Batman #1 the Joker

00:07:12.699 --> 00:07:16.830
sets off on a string of murders, announcing
his targets well in advance to terrorize the

00:07:16.830 --> 00:07:20.280
city before leaving them with their poisoned
smiles but there was one big twist.

00:07:20.560 --> 00:07:24.240
Rather than being random death and destruction,
the Joker's reason for the murders, or at

00:07:24.250 --> 00:07:28.130
least the excuse he was using, was that his
victims were keeping him from getting a patent

00:07:28.130 --> 00:07:30.289
on fish poisoned with his signature smile.

00:07:30.289 --> 00:07:33.060
It is a bizarre motive, but that's exactly
why it works.

00:07:33.240 --> 00:07:37.440
The other villains might have fixations and
obsessions, but the Joker was completely beyond

00:07:37.440 --> 00:07:37.940
reason.

00:07:37.940 --> 00:07:42.000
Demanding something so ridiculous from bureaucrats
was a challenge to the very concept of an

00:07:42.000 --> 00:07:42.540
ordered world.

00:07:42.780 --> 00:07:46.900
He quite simply could not be reasoned with,
because in his view, reason didn't even exist.

00:07:47.500 --> 00:07:51.919
Throughout the '70s and '80s, the Batman comics
were on a sort of pendulum, swinging to one

00:07:51.919 --> 00:07:55.830
extreme, gritty, violent street-level crime
dramas to the other: the pop-art camp of the

00:07:55.830 --> 00:07:56.320
'60s.

00:07:56.520 --> 00:08:00.340
He was still a superhero, but stories like
"There is No Hope In Crime Alley" and "To

00:08:00.350 --> 00:08:04.249
Kill a Legend" had pulled the focus onto the
idea of how people deal with tragedies.

00:08:04.249 --> 00:08:06.640
That same lens was applied to the villains,
too.

00:08:06.780 --> 00:08:10.500
Batman's gallery of foes went from a solid
roster of baddies with great gimmicks and

00:08:10.509 --> 00:08:14.650
interesting hooks to a group of villains who
were reworked into being specific psychological

00:08:14.650 --> 00:08:15.800
foils for the hero.

00:08:15.949 --> 00:08:19.880
Two-Face, for instance, became explicitly
cast as a tragic figure who was struggling

00:08:19.889 --> 00:08:23.469
with his own two extremes of good and evil,
and what it was like to look into the mirror

00:08:23.469 --> 00:08:25.920
and see something that was more like a mask
looking back at you.

00:08:26.100 --> 00:08:30.360
The Riddler had always had a superiority complex,
but now it was about proving that Batman,

00:08:30.360 --> 00:08:31.920
and everything he stood for, could fail.

00:08:32.090 --> 00:08:36.480
Bane explored the idea of someone with Bruce
Wayne's keen mind and gifted athleticism being

00:08:36.480 --> 00:08:40.480
born into crime, corruption, and poverty instead
of a life of privilege.

00:08:40.480 --> 00:08:43.580
The Joker was ahead of the curve, but the
same change was happening with him.

00:08:43.720 --> 00:08:48.170
He stopped being merely unpredictable, becoming
an embodiment of madness and nihilism who

00:08:48.170 --> 00:08:52.340
represented a moral threat that went far beyond
just punching him out and dragging him back

00:08:52.340 --> 00:08:53.200
to Arkham Asylum.

00:08:53.380 --> 00:08:57.040
And the thing is, he kind of had to evolve
that way, because Batman was undergoing the

00:08:57.040 --> 00:08:58.120
same change himself.

00:08:58.360 --> 00:09:01.220
"As a man, l'm flesh and blood.

00:09:01.220 --> 00:09:02.840
l can be ignored, destroyed.

00:09:02.840 --> 00:09:03.820
But as a symbol…

00:09:04.340 --> 00:09:07.400
As a symbol, l can be incorruptible."

00:09:07.500 --> 00:09:11.560
Say what you will about the "Shark-Repellent
Bat-Spray" scene in the '66 movie, but if

00:09:11.560 --> 00:09:15.650
you get right down to it, it actually is a
pretty accurate portrayal of how Batman works

00:09:15.650 --> 00:09:16.580
as a character.

00:09:16.700 --> 00:09:20.930
He's entirely built around the idea of determination,
this unbeatable will to win.

00:09:20.930 --> 00:09:25.210
He evolved from just having the tagline of
"The World's Greatest Detective" into embodying

00:09:25.210 --> 00:09:29.290
the phrase, becoming the character who thought
of everything and was always one step ahead

00:09:29.290 --> 00:09:30.240
because he had to be.

00:09:30.340 --> 00:09:32.060
Because if he wasn't, people died.

00:09:32.420 --> 00:09:34.380
And that became the consequence: death.

00:09:34.380 --> 00:09:38.330
It had always been there, going all the way
back to the origin story, but in the '80s,

00:09:38.330 --> 00:09:43.070
it was codified into the core of the character,
and as Batman's definitive arch-nemesis, the

00:09:43.070 --> 00:09:44.340
Joker evolved to embody it.

00:09:44.440 --> 00:09:45.600
He is the consequence.

00:09:46.180 --> 00:09:50.260
That's why the Joker was the one who killed
Robin, which stood for years as Batman's greatest

00:09:50.260 --> 00:09:50.760
failure.

00:09:50.900 --> 00:09:54.220
that’s why he was the central character
in The Killing Joke, where Alan Moore and

00:09:54.230 --> 00:09:59.100
Brian Bolland go back to that Red Hood story
from 1951 and rework it into its own kind

00:09:59.100 --> 00:09:59.700
of tragedy.

00:10:00.100 --> 00:10:03.540
And it's why every time he showed up after,
those consequences had to keep getting more

00:10:03.540 --> 00:10:07.460
dire, more destructive, more of an assault
on the very concepts that Batman was built

00:10:07.460 --> 00:10:07.960
on.

00:10:07.960 --> 00:10:11.640
That's how the Joker became the twisted, amoral
character that we have today.

00:10:11.640 --> 00:10:14.820
The only real question is how much more twisted
he can get.

